maintainer_reply:
When removing `--singleThread`/`--singleForks` in https://github.com/vitest-dev/vitest/pull/8705, it was assumed it's equal to `--maxThreads=1`, `--maxForks=1` and `--maxWorkers=1`. Looking at sources of v3, it also seems to disable isolation and clear modules between each test. You should achieve same with following config:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from "vitest/config";

export default defineConfig({
  test: {
    isolate: false,
    maxWorkers: 1,
    setupFiles: ["./setup.ts"],
  },
});

// setup.ts
import { beforeAll, vi } from "vitest";

beforeAll(() => {
  vi.resetModules();
  vi.restoreAllMocks();
});
```

`locator` is intentionally not exposed because we follow the principal of [testing-library](https://testing-library.com/):

> The more your tests resemble the way your software is used, the more confidence they can give you.

Using CSS to query elements is not how the user uses the app, so we intentionally restrict that. We recognize that it can be a valid way to test a library though, so you can define a simple custom locator:

```ts
import { locators } from 'vitest/browser'
locators.extend({
  getByCSS(css: string) {
    return `css=${css}`
  }
})
```

The `locator` method on locator and page is an internal API that can be changed at any point.

The v4 removed `coverage.extensions` so you'll need to configure extensions in `include` to avoid this:

```diff
import { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config';

export default defineConfig({
  test: {
    coverage: {
      enabled: true,
      provider: 'istanbul',
-      include: ['src/'],
+      include: ['src/**.{js,ts}'],
    },
  },
});
```


This change is mentioned on migration guide: https://vitest.dev/guide/migration.html#removed-options-coverage-all-and-coverage-extensions. See https://vitest.dev/guide/coverage.html#including-and-excluding-files-from-coverage-report for examples.

I didn't check the reproduction yet as there's no code to run. But got to try reproducing before applying any fixes.

That `existsSync` is intentional but it's possible that we need to move it to be done a bit later, maybe around

https://github.com/vitest-dev/vitest/blob/9b75ec5f2fa46daaaa649493eb7f643a1cc9f51a/packages/coverage-v8/src/provider.ts#L89-L91


```diff
- if (this.options.excludeAfterRemap) {
-   coverageMap.filter(filename => this.isIncluded(filename))
- }

+ coverageMap.filter(filename => {
+   const exists = existsSync(filename);
+ 
+   if (this.options.excludeAfterRemap) {
+     return exists && this.isIncluded(filename);
+   }
+ 
+   return exists;
+ })
```

> Vite plugins that provided load hooks for virtual files that may need to be processed for coverage. (virtual files may contain relevant code from actual application files. Source maps can map back to the files)

Why not load the actual source files by name, and serve files with transformed code? Like every other framework does? By treating source files as virtual files, you will run into many more issues, not just coverage. This approach doesn't sound like something you should do with Vite. 🤔 

> Reporting coverage of node package/libraries that contain source maps. (first-party libraries being common but analysis of third-party package usage is also a possibility)

Do you mean getting coverage of something that's in `node_modules`? That doesn't sound like something that should show up in project's code coverage. Feel free to open other bug report with reasoning and repro. 🙌 

> Additionally, there is the potential for performance degradation due to a synchronous file system operation being performed in a loop over every file included in the coverage. For large projects this may be problematic.

If you can point out these performance issues it would be perfect. Code coverage does so many file operations that I'd imagine `existSync` to not even show up in CPU profiles.

