maintainer_reply:
Vitest 4 supports Vite 6, but your package manager might chose the latest allowed version to install (vitest specifes ^6 || ^7).

You can add overrides (depending on your package manager) in `package.json` to keep using Vite 6 with Vitest:

npm/bun
```json
{
  "overrides": {
    "vite": "^6.4.0"
  }
}
```
yarn
```json
{
  "resolutions": {
    "vite": "^6.4.0"
  }
}
```
pnpm
```json
{
  "pnpm": {
    "overrides": {
      "vite": "^6.4.0"
    }
  }
}
```

Before Vitest 4, the spies would be reapplied when you call `vi.spyOn`. In Vitest 4 the spies are _NOT_ reapplied, the `spyOn` just returns the same spy if there is already one there.

Redux caches the values based on the function, I think, so it doesn't even call `counterSelector` when you rerender the component. In Vitest 3 it did because the function was different (`vi.spyOn` assigned a new function).

Seems like this works as expected. One thing you can do is `restore` the mock yourself before spying the second time:

```ts
test("should render string", async () => {
  const spy = vi.spyOn(counterSlice, "counterSelector").mockReturnValue(1)

  const { getByTestId, rerender } = render(
    <Provider store={store}>
      <App />
    </Provider>
  );

  expect(getByTestId("test").textContent).toEqual("1");

  spy.mockRestore() // <!-- note this
  vi.spyOn(counterSlice, "counterSelector").mockReturnValue(2);
  rerender(
    <Provider store={store}>
      <App />
    </Provider>
  );

  expect(getByTestId("test").textContent).toEqual("2");
});
```

Also I am a bit confused why you are awaiting a string, so I removed all unnecessary awaits.

