maintainer_reply:
I think this is working intended as `isolate: false` feature. I think this non-isolation behavior can be understood better by moving `mypkg/setup2` import to each test files: https://stackblitz.com/edit/vitest-dev-vitest-meynnc9k?file=test%2Fsetup.ts

```js
// [bar.test.ts]
import 'my-pkg/setup2';
...

// [foo.test.ts]
import 'my-pkg/setup2';
...
```

To benefit from such optimization feature of `isolate: false`, I think it's better not to except Vitest to invalidate module cache, but rather the setup code itself can be written in a way to avoid import side effect, for example:

```js
// [./test/setup.ts]
import { setup } from 'my-pkg/setup2-helper'
setup()

// [mypkg/setup2-helper]
export function setup() {
  afterEach(() => {
    console.log('afterEach 2 from pkg');
  });
}
```

---

That said, it's also possible to invalidate module manually by classic method: https://stackblitz.com/edit/vitest-dev-vitest-wz11gnma?file=test%2Fsetup.ts

(Note that Vite seems to strip `?t=...`, so it cannot be used as cache buster)

```ts
import { afterEach } from 'vitest';

// import 'my-pkg/setup2';
await import(import.meta.resolve('my-pkg/setup2') + '?no-cache=' + Date.now());

console.log('setup from setup.ts');

afterEach(() => {
  console.log('after Each from setup.ts');
});
```

