To me it looks 100% correct - you are not testing what happens when the condition of if-statement is falsy. In Vitest v3 the V8 coverage reports were not accurate and the reports were missing implicit-else completely https://github.com/vitest-dev/vitest/discussions/7587.

Consider following:

```js
function createUser(name) {
  const user = {};

  if(name) {
    user.name = name;
  }

  user.name = user.name.toUpperCase();

  return user;
}

test("user name is uppercased", () => {
  const user = createUser("vitest");

  expect(user.name).toBe("VITEST");
});
```

This test passes fine, but it shows 50% branch coverage as we never test what happens with `createUser(null)`. If branch coverage was 100%, it would hide actual production bug.

If we don't care about that bug, we can do `v8 ignore else` to exclude the implicit else from coverage:

```diff
  const user = {};

+ /* v8 ignore else -- @preserve */
  if(name) {
    user.name = name;
  }

  user.name = user.name.toUpperCase();
```

Your IDE (vitest vs code extension?) should probably create similar `E` marker as Vitest's coverage HTML report does.

