body:
Currently `toBeTypeOf` does not narrow the type of the `expect` argument:

```ts
let x = 'test' as string | number;
expect(x).toBeTypeOf('string');
x; // has type string | number
```

So when you want to use the narrowed type for further assertions you have to add a redundant type cast or use `expect.fail`:

```ts
if (typeof x !== 'string') {
  expect.fail(`expected string but got ${typeof x}: ${x}`);
}
// x has type string because expect.fail has the never return type
```

While you can put this into a helper function, making use of an [assertion signature](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/release-notes/typescript-3-7.html#assertion-functions):

```ts
type TypeMap = {
  string: string;
  number: number;
  boolean: boolean;
  bigint: bigint;
  symbol: symbol;
  undefined: undefined;
  object: object | null;
  function: Function;
};

export function assertType<K extends keyof TypeMap>(
  value: unknown,
  typeName: K,
): asserts value is TypeMap[K] {
  if (typeof value !== typeName) {
    expect.fail(`expected ${typeName} but got ${typeof value}: ${value}`);
  }
}
```

It would be convenient if `toBeTypeOf` just did this. (Note that this probably wouldn't work for `expect(...).not.toBeTypeOf(...)` since I don't think assertion signatures support negative assertions).

### Describe the bug

`describe.skipIf(true).for(cases)` runs the parametrized suite instead of skipping it. The same is true for any chained mode on `describe.for` (`.skip.for`, `.only.for`, `.concurrent.for`, `.sequential.for`, `.shuffle.for`, `.todo.for`, `.runIf(...).for`): the chained context is dropped and the inner suites are registered as plain `run`-mode suites.

`describe.skipIf(true).each(cases)` and plain `describe.skipIf(true)(...)` both work as expected — the bug is specific to `.for`.

### Expected

All three suites are skipped — `4 skipped`, `0 failed`.

### Actual (vitest 4.1.6)

```
 × describe.skipIf(true).for — case 1 > should be skipped but runs
 × describe.skipIf(true).for — case 2 > should be skipped but runs
 × describe.skipIf(true).for — case 3 > should be skipped but runs
 ↓ describe.skipIf(true).each — case 1 > correctly skipped
 ↓ describe.skipIf(true).each — case 2 > correctly skipped
 ↓ describe.skipIf(true).each — case 3 > correctly skipped
 ↓ describe.skipIf(true) — plain > correctly skipped

 Test Files  1 failed (1)
      Tests  3 failed | 4 skipped (7)
```

### Root cause

In `packages/runner/src/suite.ts` → `createSuite()`, `suiteFn.for` captures the outer `suite` (the unmodified module-level chainable) instead of resolving it from `this`. That means the `{ skip: true }` (or `only`, `concurrent`, …) context that `.skipIf` / the chain getters set on the chainable is silently discarded by the time `.for` registers the suites.

```js
// dist/chunk-artifact.js, createSuite()
suiteFn.for = function(cases, ...args) {
    // (no getChainableContext(this) here)
    return (name, optionsOrFn, fnOrOptions) => {
        // ...
        cases.forEach((item, idx) => {
            suite(formatTitle(name_, toArray(item), idx), options, ...);
            //  ↑ closure-bound module-level `suite`, NOT the chained one
        });
    };
};
suiteFn.skipIf = (condition) => condition ? suite.skip : suite;
```

`suiteFn.each`, just above, already does the right thing:

```js
suiteFn.each = function(cases, ...args) {
    const context = getChainableContext(this);
    const suite = context.withContext();   // chained suite
    // ...
    suite(formatTitle(...), ...);          // respects skip/only/concurrent/etc.
};
```

### Suggested fix

Mirror `.each`’s pattern in `.for`:

```diff
 suiteFn.for = function(cases, ...args) {
+    const context = getChainableContext(this);
+    const suite = context.withContext();
     if (Array.isArray(cases) && args.length) {
         cases = formatTemplateString(cases, args);
     }
     return (name, optionsOrFn, fnOrOptions) => {
         const name_ = formatName(name);
         const { options, handler } = parseArguments(optionsOrFn, fnOrOptions);
         cases.forEach((item, idx) => {
             suite(formatTitle(name_, toArray(item), idx), options, handler ? () => handler(item) : undefined);
         });
     };
 };
```

Happy to send a PR if useful.

### Reproduction

Minimal repro: https://github.com/rhwinter/vite-skipIfError/

```ts
import { describe, expect, test } from 'vitest';

const SKIP = true;
const cases = [1, 2, 3];

describe.skipIf(SKIP).for(cases)('describe.skipIf(true).for — case %s', (n) => {
  test('should be skipped but runs', () => {
    expect(n).toBe(-1);
  });
});

describe.skipIf(SKIP).each(cases)('describe.skipIf(true).each — case %s', (n) => {
  test('correctly skipped', () => {
    expect(n).toBe(-1);
  });
});

describe.skipIf(SKIP)('describe.skipIf(true) — plain', () => {
  test('correctly skipped', () => {
    expect(1).toBe(-1);
  });
});
```

