### Describe the bug

Upgrading \`vitest\` from \`4.1.5\` to \`4.1.6\` (or \`4.1.7\`) silently breaks the type augmentation that \`@testing-library/jest-dom@6.9.1\` ships at \`@testing-library/jest-dom/vitest\`. Every test that asserts a DOM matcher — \`toBeInTheDocument\`, \`toBeDisabled\`, etc. — fails \`tsc --noEmit\` with:

\`\`\`
error TS2339: Property 'toBeInTheDocument' does not exist on type 'Assertion<HTMLElement | null>'.
\`\`\`

Runtime is unaffected; the matchers execute and assert correctly. The break is purely at the type level — the \`declare module 'vitest' { interface Assertion<T = any> extends TestingLibraryMatchers<any, T> {} }\` augmentation no longer merges into the \`Assertion\` interface exported from \`vitest\`.

### Reproduction

Minimal test file:

\`\`\`ts
// foo.test.tsx
import { render } from "@testing-library/react";
import "@testing-library/jest-dom/vitest";
import { describe, expect, it } from "vitest";

describe("foo", () => {
  it("renders", () => {
    const { container } = render(<div data-testid="x" />);
    expect(container.querySelector("[data-testid=x]")).toBeInTheDocument();
    //                                                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    // TS2339 on vitest 4.1.6+, fine on 4.1.5
  });
});
\`\`\`

Run \`tsc --noEmit\` against the file with each vitest version:

| vitest | typecheck |
|---|---|
| 4.1.5 | ✓ |
| 4.1.6 | ✗ |
| 4.1.7 | ✗ |

### Relevant jest-dom augmentation file

\`node_modules/@testing-library/jest-dom/types/vitest.d.ts\`:

\`\`\`ts
import 'vitest'
import {type TestingLibraryMatchers} from './matchers'

declare module 'vitest' {
  interface Assertion<T = any>
    extends TestingLibraryMatchers<any, T> {}
  interface AsymmetricMatchersContaining
    extends TestingLibraryMatchers<any, any> {}
}
\`\`\`

In vitest 4.1.5 this merges into the \`Assertion\` interface exported by the \`vitest\` package. In 4.1.6 the merge silently no-ops — the \`interface Assertion\` slot in \`module 'vitest'\` is no longer reachable by external augmentation.

### What I think changed (not verified)

The GitHub diff [\`v4.1.5...v4.1.6\`](https://github.com/vitest-dev/vitest/compare/v4.1.5...v4.1.6) shows **no source changes** in \`packages/expect/\` — only a version bump in \`package.json\`. So either:

- A change elsewhere in the monorepo (\`packages/runner\`, \`packages/utils\`, \`packages/vitest\`) re-shaped how \`Assertion\` is re-exported from the top-level \`vitest\` package
- The 4.1.6 build pipeline regenerated dist outputs differently from 4.1.5 (different rollup-dts settings, declaration flattening, etc.)

I haven't bisected past the npm-published artifacts.

### Impact

Any project that uses \`@testing-library/jest-dom/vitest\` (the documented integration path) cannot upgrade past vitest 4.1.5 without losing matcher typechecking on every DOM test. We're holding vitest at \`^4.1.5\` in [opendigitalproductfactory/opendigitalproductfactory#871](https://github.com/OpenDigitalProductFactory/opendigitalproductfactory/pull/871) until this resolves.

### System Info

\`\`\`
vitest 4.1.5 / 4.1.6 / 4.1.7
@vitest/expect (matched vitest version)
@testing-library/jest-dom 6.9.1
typescript 6.0.3
node 24.13.1
Windows 11 / pnpm 10.33.2
\`\`\`

### Used Package Manager

pnpm

### Validations

- I confirmed this is not an issue with a third-party library (the augmentation file exists and is well-formed; the only variable is vitest's published type surface).
- I bisected between 4.1.5 (works) and 4.1.6 (broken).
- I searched issues with terms "jest-dom", "toBeInTheDocument", "Assertion augmentation" and did not find an existing open report for this regression.

Happy to provide a runnable repro repo if useful.

### Clear and concise description of the problem

As a developer using Vitest I want `coverage.thresholds.autoUpdate` to receive
the previous threshold as a second argument so that I can tell apart V8 variance
from a real regression.

The current callback signature is `(newValue: number) => number`. Without the
old value there is no way to write logic like "only accept a drop of max X%"

### Suggested solution

Pass the current (pre-update) threshold as a second argument to the `autoUpdate`
callback. The value is already in scope inside `updateThresholds` as `thresholds[key]` — it just isn't forwarded to the formatter. The change is a single line and fully backwards-compatible (existing single-arg callbacks continue to work).


```ts
// packages/vitest/src/node/coverage.ts — updateThresholds()
// before
const formattedValue = thresholdFormatter(actual)
// after
const formattedValue = thresholdFormatter(actual, threshold ?? 100)
```

This enables a drift-tolerance pattern:
```ts
// vitest.config.ts
coverage: {
  thresholds: {
    autoUpdate: (newValue, oldValue) => {
      if ((oldValue - newValue) > X) {
        throw new Error(`Coverage dropped ${(oldValue - newValue).toFixed(1)}% — intentional?`)
      }
      return Math.max(0, Math.floor(newValue) - X)
    }
  }
}
```

### Alternative

_No response_

### Additional context

In my experience V8's native coverage can produce slightly different numbers between runs, which is why a fixed buffer feels necessary. Is this something others run into too? We would love to hear what buffer values people are using in practice.

I looked at the source and the change appears small enough that we would be happy to open a PR if the maintainers agree with the direction. The old threshold is already in scope when `thresholdsToUpdate` is built — it just needs to be
carried through to the formatter call, and the `autoUpdate` type signature updated to `(newValue: number, oldValue: number) => number`. 
Is that the right approach?

### Validations

- [x] Follow our [Code of Conduct](https://github.com/vitest-dev/vitest/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)
- [x] Read the [Contributing Guidelines](https://github.com/vitest-dev/vitest/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md).
- [x] Read the [docs](https://vitest.dev/guide/).
- [x] Check that there isn't already an issue that requests the same feature to avoid creating a duplicate.

maintainer_reply:
It is a constraint of TypeScript language. Any assertion function needs to be annotated. What you have to do is declare it with `Chai.Assert` or use `import.meta.vitest.assert` directly without destructuring:

```ts
if (import.meta.vitest) {
  const { test } = import.meta.vitest
  const assert: Chai.Assert = import.meta.vitest.assert

  test('add', () => {
    const value = add(1, 2)
    import.meta.vitest!.assert(value)
    assert(value)
  })
}
```

I think this does deserve a place in the documentation. PR welcome

