### Describe the bug

When mocking a **class constructor** with `vi.spyOn(obj, 'key').mockImplementation(SomeClass)`, instances created from the mocked constructor lose all their methods — `instance.method` is `undefined`.

I am using `vi.spyOn()` to replace a class held on an object property, passing a replacement class (which `extends` the original and overrides a method) to `.mockImplementation()`. I expect `new obj.Key()` to return an instance of the replacement class, so that `instance.method()` runs the overridden implementation and returns `"mocked"`. What actually happens is that the created instance has no methods at all: `typeof instance.method` is `"undefined"` instead of `"function"`.

This is explicitly documented as a supported use case. The [`vi.spyOn` docs](https://vitest.dev/api/vi.html#vi-spyon) state:

> If the spying method is a class definition, the mock implementations have to use the `function` or the `class` keyword:

The reproduction below uses the `class` keyword exactly as the documentation prescribes, yet the resulting instance has no methods.

```ts
import { expect, test, vi } from "vitest";

test("vi.spyOn with class mockImplementation bug", () => {
  class OriginalClass {
    method() {
      return "original";
    }
  }

  const myObj = { TestClass: OriginalClass };

  vi.spyOn(myObj, "TestClass").mockImplementation(
    class MockClass extends OriginalClass {
      method() {
        return "mocked";
      }
    },
  );

  const instance = new myObj.TestClass();

  expect(typeof instance.method).toBe("function"); // ❌ received "undefined"
  expect(instance.method()).toBe("mocked");
});
```

**Expected:** the test passes — `instance.method` is a function returning `"mocked"`.

**Actual:** the test fails:

```
AssertionError: expected 'undefined' to be 'function' // Object.is equality

Expected: "function"
Received: "undefined"

